1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,263
Think about a list you already use:

2
00:00:02,810 --> 00:00:07,430
contacts, places to visit,
or resources for a community activity.

3
00:00:08,370 --> 00:00:10,690
We will turn a fictional
resource list into

4
00:00:10,690 --> 00:00:11,510
a small browser

5
00:00:11,510 --> 00:00:16,310
app that helps someone find an entry.
Your experience with lists gives

6
00:00:16,310 --> 00:00:20,041
you a useful starting point for
deciding whether the result makes sense.

7
00:00:20,750 --> 00:00:23,950
You do not need to become a
professional programmer today.

8
00:00:24,510 --> 00:00:26,375
You will practice directing a build,

9
00:00:26,670 --> 00:00:30,310
checking what it does,
and making one controlled improvement.

10
00:00:31,350 --> 00:00:32,970
The first version has one page,

11
00:00:33,370 --> 00:00:37,350
a search field, a category filter,
and readable results.

12
00:00:38,050 --> 00:00:39,590
Keep the records fictional so

13
00:00:39,590 --> 00:00:43,735
the work can be shared and tested without
exposing anyone's private information.

14
00:00:45,746 --> 00:00:48,526
A website can present
information for people to read.

15
00:00:49,166 --> 00:00:53,166
A web app responds to input,
such as filtering our resource list.

16
00:00:53,866 --> 00:00:55,046
Software as a service

17
00:00:55,046 --> 00:00:58,846
usually provides an ongoing hosted
service and may include accounts,

18
00:00:59,526 --> 00:01:02,426
stored data, subscriptions,
and operational support.

19
00:01:03,306 --> 00:01:05,846
Our one-file practice
app is not all of that.

20
00:01:06,266 --> 00:01:09,886
It has no real account system,
private database, or payment service.

21
00:01:10,626 --> 00:01:13,966
This distinction helps you ask
for an achievable first version.

22
00:01:13,966 --> 00:01:19,146
A convincing sign-in screen does not prove
secure authentication exists behind it.

23
00:01:19,626 --> 00:01:23,616
Start by describing the useful behavior
you can actually test in the browser,

24
00:01:24,266 --> 00:01:25,846
then treat hosting, security,

25
00:01:26,406 --> 00:01:30,146
and service operations as separate
decisions that require more work.

26
00:01:31,925 --> 00:01:34,345
HTML describes the content and structure:

27
00:01:35,065 --> 00:01:38,465
a heading, a labeled search field,
and a list of resources.

28
00:01:39,505 --> 00:01:42,565
CSS controls how those parts
are arranged and displayed.

29
00:01:43,465 --> 00:01:47,465
JavaScript responds to input, such as
deciding which records match a search.

30
00:01:48,665 --> 00:01:52,240
Think of a familiar form: the
questions and fields are its structure,

31
00:01:52,565 --> 00:01:54,586
their presentation helps you read them,

32
00:01:54,645 --> 00:01:57,391
and the rules determine what
happens when you use it.

33
00:01:58,165 --> 00:02:01,896
The analogy is a starting point,
not a complete description of software.

34
00:02:02,545 --> 00:02:03,405
Open the supplied

35
00:02:03,405 --> 00:02:07,025
starter in the browser and identify
a visible example of each part.

36
00:02:08,005 --> 00:02:11,995
Changing a heading is different from
changing the rule that filters the list.

37
00:02:13,855 --> 00:02:17,215
Define success in terms
someone else can observe.

38
00:02:17,755 --> 00:02:22,055
First, searching for library should show
the fictional Community library entry.

39
00:02:22,855 --> 00:02:27,475
Second, searching for an unmatched term
should show a clear no-results message.

40
00:02:28,555 --> 00:02:31,146
Third, the controls should
work with the keyboard.

41
00:02:31,735 --> 00:02:34,875
Add a check for different letter
cases and a narrow screen.

42
00:02:35,595 --> 00:02:36,935
These are acceptance checks:

43
00:02:37,295 --> 00:02:41,233
statements about behavior that help you
decide whether the result is usable.

44
00:02:42,235 --> 00:02:43,995
Avoid a requirement such as make

45
00:02:43,995 --> 00:02:47,435
it amazing,
because it does not explain what to test.

46
00:02:48,675 --> 00:02:52,235
Pause and write three checks for
a small app idea of your own.

47
00:02:53,875 --> 00:02:55,155
Ask a partner whether

48
00:02:55,155 --> 00:02:58,471
they could carry out each check
without guessing what you meant.

49
00:03:00,103 --> 00:03:02,083
A useful prompt describes the user,

50
00:03:02,483 --> 00:03:06,003
the task, the controls,
the data boundaries, and the checks.

51
00:03:06,723 --> 00:03:07,473
For example:

52
00:03:07,523 --> 00:03:08,423
build one local

53
00:03:08,423 --> 00:03:13,103
HTML page that searches fictional
community resources by name and category.

54
00:03:13,723 --> 00:03:14,803
Include labeled fields,

55
00:03:15,383 --> 00:03:19,823
visible keyboard focus, a no-results
message, and no external dependencies.

56
00:03:20,663 --> 00:03:24,135
Keep all example records fictional
and explain how to run the file.

57
00:03:24,423 --> 00:03:28,303
Ask the AI to describe the important
parts so you can inspect its work.

58
00:03:28,563 --> 00:03:33,283
Do not put passwords or private API keys
into the prompt or the browser code.

59
00:03:33,663 --> 00:03:34,783
People can inspect code

60
00:03:34,783 --> 00:03:36,051
delivered to their browser.

61
00:03:36,303 --> 00:03:38,583
A longer prompt is not
automatically better;

62
00:03:38,723 --> 00:03:42,350
each instruction should clarify
behavior or a boundary you can verify.

63
00:03:44,122 --> 00:03:45,982
Use the instructor-approved tool if

64
00:03:45,982 --> 00:03:48,728
one is available,
or begin with the supplied starter.

65
00:03:49,522 --> 00:03:53,022
Save the file with its HTML
extension and open it in the browser.

66
00:03:53,982 --> 00:03:57,762
Confirm that the heading, search field,
filter, and results appear.

67
00:03:58,562 --> 00:04:01,567
Keep this first working version
before asking for changes.

68
00:04:03,302 --> 00:04:05,322
If no AI account is available,

69
00:04:05,922 --> 00:04:09,083
you can still complete the
exercise with a plain-text editor:

70
00:04:09,322 --> 00:04:11,705
change the heading and
one fictional resource,

71
00:04:11,942 --> 00:04:13,963
save the file, and refresh the browser.

72
00:04:14,662 --> 00:04:18,393
Be careful that the editor has not
silently added a text-file extension.

73
00:04:19,042 --> 00:04:21,374
The goal is a working
result you can explain,

74
00:04:21,882 --> 00:04:25,094
not evidence that you used a
particular paid service or model.

75
00:04:26,934 --> 00:04:27,914
Type library into

76
00:04:27,914 --> 00:04:31,696
the search field and compare the result
with your first acceptance check.

77
00:04:31,934 --> 00:04:35,034
Then enter an unmatched term such as zzz.

78
00:04:35,534 --> 00:04:36,494
The page should

79
00:04:36,494 --> 00:04:37,154
explain that there

80
00:04:37,154 --> 00:04:41,299
are no matching resources rather than
appearing broken or showing an old result.

81
00:04:41,854 --> 00:04:44,954
Clear the field and try
LIBRARY in uppercase.

82
00:04:45,594 --> 00:04:48,654
If the intended behavior is
a case-insensitive search,

83
00:04:49,274 --> 00:04:50,729
it should find the same record.

84
00:04:51,534 --> 00:04:52,614
Next combine the search

85
00:04:52,614 --> 00:04:53,594
with a category filter

86
00:04:53,594 --> 00:04:54,554
and inspect whether

87
00:04:54,554 --> 00:04:59,654
both conditions apply. Record expected
and actual results separately.

88
00:05:01,294 --> 00:05:04,414
A confident statement from
the AI is not test evidence.

89
00:05:05,314 --> 00:05:05,974
The evidence is

90
00:05:05,974 --> 00:05:09,446
what happened when you carried out
a named check on the saved file.

91
00:05:11,023 --> 00:05:14,546
Set the mouse aside and move through
the controls with the keyboard.

92
00:05:15,123 --> 00:05:16,988
Can you see which control has focus?

93
00:05:17,863 --> 00:05:20,503
Can you enter a search
and operate the filter?

94
00:05:21,463 --> 00:05:23,950
Narrow the browser window
and check that labels,

95
00:05:24,283 --> 00:05:28,783
results, and buttons remain readable
without important content being cut off.

96
00:05:29,883 --> 00:05:31,291
Then ask where the data lives.

97
00:05:31,863 --> 00:05:34,661
In our starter,
fictional records are inside the file.

98
00:05:35,123 --> 00:05:35,874
Browser storage,

99
00:05:36,123 --> 00:05:39,823
if added later, may remain only in
that browser and may be cleared;

100
00:05:39,823 --> 00:05:43,463
it is not automatically a
shared database or a backup.

101
00:05:44,363 --> 00:05:47,063
Test persistence rather than assuming it.

102
00:05:47,723 --> 00:05:48,503
Use the course's

103
00:05:48,503 --> 00:05:53,023
existing text-size and accessibility
habits as standards for the app you build.

104
00:05:54,810 --> 00:05:56,675
Suppose the uppercase search failed.

105
00:05:57,650 --> 00:06:00,730
A useful revision request
names the observed problem,

106
00:06:01,430 --> 00:06:03,570
the expected behavior, and the check:

107
00:06:04,350 --> 00:06:06,430
LIBRARY returns no result,

108
00:06:06,430 --> 00:06:08,750
but library returns Community library;

109
00:06:09,710 --> 00:06:13,530
make both searches match the same
entry and preserve the category filter.

110
00:06:14,630 --> 00:06:17,310
Save the revised version
under a distinct name.

111
00:06:18,130 --> 00:06:21,730
Repeat the failed check, then repeat
a check that previously passed.

112
00:06:21,730 --> 00:06:27,370
This second step can reveal a regression,
where fixing one behavior breaks another.

113
00:06:28,730 --> 00:06:32,770
If the new version is worse,
return to the saved working version.

114
00:06:34,030 --> 00:06:36,930
Pause and make one small
improvement or repair.

115
00:06:37,990 --> 00:06:42,490
Keep a brief test log so a partner can
follow what changed and why you trust it.

116
00:06:44,193 --> 00:06:47,973
Show a partner the saved app and carry
out your three acceptance checks.

117
00:06:48,513 --> 00:06:53,573
Explain one part of the page, one change
you made, and one limitation that remains.

118
00:06:54,653 --> 00:06:55,513
A local fictional

119
00:06:55,513 --> 00:06:56,473
prototype can

120
00:06:56,473 --> 00:07:00,618
be a successful learning result without
being ready to operate a public service.

121
00:07:01,413 --> 00:07:02,853
Hosting is a separate choice.

122
00:07:03,573 --> 00:07:05,283
A real service handling accounts,

123
00:07:05,753 --> 00:07:08,499
private data,
or payments needs appropriate security,

124
00:07:09,133 --> 00:07:12,733
access controls, support,
and testing beyond this exercise.

125
00:07:13,693 --> 00:07:16,493
Do not treat a decorative
sign-in form as protection.

126
00:07:17,793 --> 00:07:20,973
Carry forward the method you practiced:
define a small task,

127
00:07:21,613 --> 00:07:22,740
give clear instructions,

128
00:07:23,473 --> 00:07:24,365
inspect the result,

129
00:07:24,733 --> 00:07:28,953
test more than the easiest path,
and keep a way back when you revise it.
